{"folioDeck":1,"exportedAt":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","meta":{"id":"faabc","title":"Persian Alphabet","subtitle":"43 letters and signs · both directions, as two cards per letter","desc":"The Persian alphabet: 43 notes — the thirty-two letters in the alphabet's order (the twenty-eight of Arabic and the four Persian added, پ چ ژ گ), each followed by what it does that its name does not say (the silent و of خواهر, the final ه that is a vowel, the final ی read â), then آ, the three short-vowel marks, the doubling mark, the hamza, the -an ending of لطفاً and the half-space of می‌روم. The first deck of the Persian collection. Both directions: see a letter and recall its sound, or see a sound and recall the letter, each a card with its own schedule. AT THE DEFAULT PACE of ten new cards a day the 43 notes are 86 cards and take 9 days; A1 can be started beside it, since every word card prints its romanisation. Persian is written right to left, and most letters join the letters on both sides, changing shape as they do, so each letter is shown in its four shapes — alone, and at the start, middle and end of a word; the seven that never join the letter after them (ا د ذ ر ز ژ و) say so. Every note gives the romanisation the level decks use, the sound compared with an English word, and one everyday word that shows it, with a speaker beside the letter and the word. Persian spells some sounds with more than one letter — s with س ث ص, z with ز ذ ض ظ, t with ت ط, h with ه ح, and in Tehran one sound for غ and ق — and each such card names the others; the sound side names the word it asks for, so every card has one answer. THE SHORT VOWELS ARE USUALLY NOT WRITTEN, which is why the level decks romanise every word: four A1 words in five have a vowel the script does not show. The sounds are those of modern Iranian (Tehran) Persian. Sources. The English comparisons are the Farsi cells of Wikipedia's Help:IPA/Persian (revision 1379048408), as are the groups of letters with one sound; the four added letters, the seven that do not join, the silent alef, the final ه, tanvin and the half-space are sentences of Wikipedia's \"Persian alphabet\" (revision 1385657500), both CC BY-SA 4.0; the example words' romanisations are English Wiktionary's, via the kaikki.org extraction (CC BY-SA 4.0).","author":"","language":"en","color":"#008763","tags":["persian","farsi","alphabet"],"glossMode":"site","types":{"fa-abc":{"id":"fa-abc","name":"Persian letter","speechLang":"fa-IR","fields":["Letter","Name","Shapes","Sound","Clue","Example","Word"],"cards":[{"name":"Letter → sound","front":"<div class=\"uc-letter\">{{Letter}}<span class=\"uc-tts uc-say\" data-say=\"{{Name}}\"></span></div>{{#Shapes}}<div class=\"uc-shapes\">{{Shapes}}</div>{{/Shapes}}","back":"{{FrontSide}}<hr><div class=\"uc-field\"><div class=\"uc-pos\">sound</div><div class=\"uc-gl\">{{Sound}}</div><div class=\"uc-pos uc-gap\">as in</div><div class=\"uc-gl\">{{Example}}<span class=\"uc-tts uc-say\" data-say=\"{{Word}}\"></span></div></div>"},{"name":"Sound → letter","front":"<div class=\"uc-ask\">Which letter is this?</div><div class=\"uc-field\"><div class=\"uc-gl\">{{Sound}}</div><div class=\"uc-pos uc-gap\">the letter in</div><div class=\"uc-gl\">{{Clue}}</div></div>","back":"{{FrontSide}}<hr><div class=\"uc-letter\">{{Letter}}<span class=\"uc-tts uc-say\" data-say=\"{{Name}}\"></span></div>{{#Shapes}}<div class=\"uc-shapes\">{{Shapes}}</div>{{/Shapes}}<div class=\"uc-field\"><div class=\"uc-pos\">sound</div><div class=\"uc-gl\">{{Sound}}</div><div class=\"uc-pos uc-gap\">as in</div><div class=\"uc-gl\">{{Example}}<span class=\"uc-tts uc-say\" data-say=\"{{Word}}\"></span></div></div>"}],"css":".card {\n  text-align: center;\n  font-size: 17px;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n.uc-ask {\n  margin-bottom: 14px;\n  font-size: 11px;\n  letter-spacing: 0.14em;\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  opacity: 0.55;\n}\n.uc-letter {\n  font-size: 64px;\n  font-weight: 400;\n  line-height: 1.3;\n}\n.uc-say {\n  margin-lef